      "narrative": "Mara's eyebrow lifts at your question \u2014 a flicker of respect crossing her face. She leans forward, voice dropping even lower.\n\n\"That's the right question to ask.\"\n\nShe glances toward the corner table where **Toran Vex** sits, still shuffling his papers. The clerk's ink-stained fingers pause mid-motion when he feels the weight of her gaze.\n\n\"Toran,\" Mara calls, not loudly, but with a note of command. \"Come here.\"\n\nThe clerk rises reluctantly, clutching a sheaf of papers to his chest like a shield. He approaches, eyes darting between you and Mara.\n\n**Toran Vex** clears his throat. \"I\u2014 I was the lockwright, yes. For the Ash Gate. For seven years, I maintained the mechanism. Every month, oiled the hinges, checked the tumblers, tested the seal.\" His voice is thin, reedy. \"It was... routine. Until it wasn't.\"\n\nMara picks up the thread. \"Toran was the last person to touch the lock before it was sealed. He's the one who filed it shut on the council's orders.\"\n\nToran's hands tremble slightly. \"I didn't want to. But the council\u2014 they were terrified. And the key...\" He swallows. \"The master key was lost the night Bram died. Without it, the lock couldn't be turned. Filing it was the only way to make sure nothing got *through*.\"\n\nHe looks at you, something raw in his expression. \"But here's the thing I've never told anyone \u2014 the lock wasn't broken when I filed it. It was *already locked*. From the inside.\""
    },
