      "narrative": "You crouch beneath the arch, fingers brushing the wet cobbles. Your eyes trace every crack, every shadow, every irregularity in the stone. The black petals \u2014 or ash, or whatever they are \u2014 cling to the damp surface, smearing under your touch like crushed charcoal. You scrape at a seam between two stones, peer into the gap where the arch meets the ground, turn over a few loose pebbles.\n\nNothing.\n\nJust wet stone, mud, and the stubborn scatter of those dark fragments. No hidden catch, no buried token, no message scratched into the rock. The ground beneath the Gate is as mute as the Gate itself.\n\nYou're still crouched there, hand pressed to the cold cobbles, when a sound cuts through the silence \u2014 the rustle of fabric. The woman has risen to her feet.\n\nShe takes two steps toward you, boots clicking on the wet stone. Her hand rests on the strap of her courier's satchel.\n\n\"I told you. No one passes through. No one passes back.\" Her voice carries an edge now \u2014 not hostile, but worn thin. \"I've been here three days watching this Gate. You think I'd miss something hidden under my own feet?\"\n\nShe stops a few paces away, hood still shadowing her face.\n\n\"My name is **Sable Quill**. I was Bram's courier. I carried the messages that went through this Gate and the replies that came back.\" A pause. \"If you want to know what's hidden here, you need to stop scraping at stones and start asking the right questions.\"\n\nShe tilts her head.\n\n\"Like: who gave the order to seal it. And why they killed Bram to make sure it stayed that way.\""
    },
