      "narrative": "The walk back passes in a grey blur. The narrow street, the leaning buildings, the damp smell \u2014 all the same, but heavier now, weighted with what you've learned. The Ash Gate's silence follows you like a second shadow.\n\nThe Copper Finch's door groans as you push it open. Warmth washes over you \u2014 the hearth crackling, the smell of old ale and woodsmoke. A few patrons nurse morning cups at scattered tables, heads bent over their drinks. The place is quieter than before, the morning crowd thin.\n\nYour table \u2014 the one against the back wall, scarred wood, a view of the whole room \u2014 sits empty. A half-melted candle stub in a tin holder marks where you left it.\n\nAs you settle into the chair, the wood creaks familiarly under you. The hearth pops. A spoon clinks against a bowl from the kitchen.\n\nMoments later, **Mara Venn** emerges from the back room, a steaming mug in each hand. She crosses to your table without hesitation, sets one down before you \u2014 dark tea, honeyed \u2014 and takes the seat across.\n\n\"Didn't expect you back so soon.\" She wraps her hands around her own mug, studying you. \"The Gate tell you anything worth hearing?\""
